title: set_field corrupts a value containing a backslash
created: 2026-09-18T18:36:27Z
updated: 2026-09-18T20:37:59Z
effort: S
area: cli
handoff:

set_field passes the value through awk -v, which processes escape sequences.
So a project field holding a backslash is silently changed on the way in:

  pidly set 2 note 'a \t and \\ here'
  pidly show -f note 2
  a <TAB> and \ here

Measured 2026-09-18. The value is corrupted in the file, not just in the
display, and nothing reports it: the line is still a legal field, check has
nothing to say, and the loss is only visible to whoever knows what they typed.

48 hit the same bug in set_line, which writes the format's own lines, and
fixed it there by passing the line through the environment instead:

  PIDLY_LINE=$3; export PIDLY_LINE
  awk -v n="$_sl_n" 'NR==n{print ENVIRON["PIDLY_LINE"]; next}{print}'

set_field needs the same treatment, and it is slightly more work because the
awk builds the slot itself — it decides between "k:" and "k: v" and has to
find the line to replace — so the value has to reach that logic through
ENVIRON rather than being interpolated into it.

Worth checking the same pattern everywhere else while in there: any awk -v
carrying a value that came from a user rather than from pidly. append_body
and set_body take text through stdin and a here-document, so they are likely
fine, but likely is not checked.

Plausible in practice rather than theoretical: this tracker's own tickets
quote shell and awk constantly, and a field is one line of free text.

Done when a value containing \t, \\ and a trailing backslash survives a
write and a read unchanged, every other awk -v carrying user text is
audited, and the suite covers it.


Done 2026-09-18. Three call sites, one of them the reported bug and two found
by the audit the ticket asked for.

set_field. The reported one. Key and value now travel through the
environment, so a value holding \t, \\ or a trailing backslash reaches the
file as typed. Verified against the previous build, which stored 'a \t and \\
here' as a tab and a single backslash, silently, with check content because
the line was still a legal field.

get_field. Found while in there, and a different fault in the same function
pair. index($0, k ":") finds the line literally, then sub("^" k ": *") cut it
with a regex built from the same key — so the two disagreed whenever the key
held a metacharacter. A hand-written field

  a+b: the value

is found by index and then not matched by /^a+b: */, so get_field returned the
whole line, key included. Measured on the previous build. It is substr now,
which is what cmd_field already did, so the two readers finally agree.

Worth being precise about what this was not: index() gates the match, so a
key like "a.b" never matched a line named "axb". The first version of this
fix claimed it did; the failure is the cut, not the find.

pidly agents. Latent rather than live: the generated block goes through
awk -v, and it holds no backslash today. The day one is added, awk -v would
rewrite it on the way into AGENTS.md — and check compares the file against
what would be generated now, so both sides would agree and the document would
simply be wrong, with nothing to notice it. Through the environment now too.

Audited and left alone, because none of them carry user text: the awks taking
a ticket number, a root path, declared field names from the schema, the
format's own field list, and the begin/end markers. append_body and set_body
were the other candidates the ticket named, and they take text through stdin
and a here-document, so they never had the problem.

Covered in set.sh: four values through a write and a read — escapes,
a trailing backslash, regex and awk metacharacters, and a literal tab — and
a hand-written field name holding a metacharacter being cut correctly.
